  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">STRENGTHENING ECOLOGICAL CONNECTIVITY THROUGH THE CREATION AND EXPANSION OF COMMUNAL CONSERVATION AREAS IN THE TROPICAL ANDES OF NORTHERN PERU</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THIS PROJECT WILL SUPPORT CONSERVATION EFFORTS IN NORTHERN PERU TO EXPAND AND STRENGTHEN A CONSERVATION CORRIDOR SPANNING APPROXIMATELY 228,000 HECTARES (563,000 ACRES) OF HUMID MONTANE FORESTS, CLOUD FORESTS AND PRAMOS (ALPINE TUNDRA ECOSYSTEMS LOCATED IN THE ANDES MOUNTAINS OF SOUTH AMERICA) THAT BORDER ALREADY ESTABLISHED PROTECTED AREAS THAT COVER APPROXIMATELY 50 OF THE AREA. THE UNPROTECTED AREAS LIE IN THE CENTRAL AND SOUTHERN SECTIONS OF THE CORRIDOR, OFFERING THE OPPORTUNITY TO FORM A CONTINUOUS ZONE OF ECOSYSTEMS IN THEIR NATURAL STATE TO MAINTAIN THE ECOLOGICAL CONNECTIVITY OF THE ENTIRE CORRIDOR. THE CORRIDOR IS LOCATED AT THE HEADWATERS OF NUMEROUS WATERSHEDS, AND ITS FORESTS AND PRAMOS FUNCTION AS CATCHMENT AND REGULATION AREAS FOR DOWNSTREAM POPULATIONS. THE MAIN THREATS IDENTIFIED IN THE AREA ARE DEFORESTATION, HABITAT DEGRADATION, AND ILLEGAL CONSTRUCTION OFROADS, ALL OF WHICH HAVE HAD NEGATIVE IMPACTS ON THE ECOSYSTEM SERVICES PROVIDED BY THE FORESTS AND PRAMOS.IES OF THREATS.</narrative>
  </description>
